   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Achieving Indonesia&apos;s goal of net zero emissions by 2060 will require backing from the financial sector. The Indonesian Financial Services Authority (OJK) has set a target to accelerate sustainable finance through the Indonesian sustainable finance taxonomy as a pathway to low-emissions development. 
&#x200d;
Indonesia&#x2019;s sustainable finance taxonomy &#x2013; a system to identify and classify economic activities &#x2013; was launched on 20 January 2024. It serves as a reference for financial services providers, investors and businesses (at the national and international levels) to disclose information related to financing, funding or investment in green economic activities. The first version of the taxonomy focused on energy and critical minerals extraction &#x2013; areas significant to the renewable energy transition.
&#x200d;
Australia coordinated with OJK on the Indonesian sustainable finance taxonomy, as well as the ASEAN equivalent, and provided technical inputs on both mechanisms. Key partners include the Australian Treasury and the Australian Sustainable Finance Institute.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported Indonesia&#x2019;s Health Minister set the strategy for the transformation, translated the vision into actionable initiatives and facilitated organisational change. The transformation focuses on six areas of the health system: primary care, secondary care, system resilience, financing, workforce, and technology. It includes specific measures to improve maternal and child health outcomes and to reduce regional inequalities in secondary care through improving access to sub-national hospitals. The Ministry of Health secured funding from several international development banks totalling approximately USD 4 billion ( AUD 5.9 billion) over four years (2022-23 &#x2013; 2025-26) based on Australia&#x2019;s support for a clear transformation plan and roadmap.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the Indonesian Government to accelerating the electronic-based government system (SPBE) initiative to make public services more efficient and improve citizen access. Led by the Ministry of Administrative and Bureaucratic Reform and the Ministry of Communication and Information Technology, SPBE aims to integrate nine priority services &#x2013; including health, education and social protection &#x2013; into a single portal. The Presidential Regulation on the Acceleration of Digital Transformation and Integration of National Digital Services was signed in December 2023, informed by Australian insights on citizen-centric digital social services. At the SPBE Summit in May 2024, President Joko Widodo launched Govtech Indonesia (INA Digital) which is tasked to integrate digital services. 
&#x200d;
Australia supported the development of a digital governance framework, whole-of-government engagement, policy dialogue and government-to-government knowledge sharing with the Australian Public Service Commission, Digital Transformation Agency, Services Australia and Department of Finance.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In August 2023, the Indonesian Ministry of Health initiated the second phase of the health transformation agenda and enacted the Omnibus Health Law (No. 12/2023) which provides legislative solidity and certainty to the reform process and streamlines 11 existing laws.
&#x200d;
As a trusted partner, Australia assisted with the design and early implementation of the second 12-month phase of the health transformation. Priorities included strengthening Indonesia&#x2019;s health financing mechanisms, accelerating the integration of primary care services, forecasting health workforce requirements, and enabling the Ministry of Health&#x2019;s internal transformation. Additionally, Australia&#x2019;s earlier diagnostics and advice provided greater clarity and direction for the necessary legislative reforms under the Omnibus Health Law.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In October 2023, Indonesia became the 40th member of the Financial Action Task Force (FATF). For more than 10 years, Australia through the Australian Transaction Reports and Analysis Centre (AUSTRAC) and Australian financial agencies supported the Indonesian Financial Transaction Reports and Analysis Centre (PPATK) to strengthen Indonesia&#x2019;s anti-money laundering and countering terrorist financing regimes consistent with international standards. This culminated in positive findings by FATF on improvements to Indonesia&#x2019;s legal and regulatory framework since 2012 (when it was considered a high-risk jurisdiction) and final recommendations issued in February 2023. 
&#x200d;
In 2023, Australia supported Indonesia in responding to outstanding issues based on the FATF recommendations. Specific Australian support included capacity building, IT development, knowledge sharing, strategic national integration, and risk management. This is an important economic reform that will provide confidence in the financial system underpinning Indonesia&#x2019;s growth potential.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indonesia&#x2019;s accession to the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) reflects its growing stature as a major global economy and supports the country&#x2019;s Vision 2045 ambition to become an advanced economy. The accession process will drive domestic reform, including by helping Indonesia access and apply strong technical and policy standards to guide its institutional strengthening.
&#x200d;
Since the adoption of the OECD accession roadmap for Indonesia in May 2024, Indonesia has submitted its Initial Memorandum to the OECD and established institutional arrangements to drive a coordinated, whole-of-government approach. A Regulatory Gap Assessment evaluated Indonesia&#x2019;s alignment with OECD standards, with the OECD recommending reforms across 32 technical areas.
&#x200d;
Prospera and its Australian Public Service partners have supported Indonesia throughout this process. Prospera contributed to the gap assessment and established an integrated process control system to facilitate inter-ministerial communication, document sharing, and progress monitoring. The Australian Treasury supported the Ministry of Finance in developing the Initial Memorandum and facilitated virtual introductory meetings with relevant OECD committees. The Australian Bureau of Statistics assisted Statistics Indonesia as a critical friend with a peer review of accession documentation and regularly met to discuss accession plans including participation on international committees.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indonesia&#x2019;s successful year as ASEAN Chair concluded on 31 December 2023. The agenda focused on economic issues with global relevance identified as priority deliverables and covered three strategic areas: recovery and rebuilding, digital economy, and sustainability. 
&#x200d;
Key achievements included deepening regional integration on the digital economy, low-carbon development, and food security. This included the ASEAN Blue Economy Framework and an update to the ASEAN Sustainable Taxonomy. Other notable developments included progress on implementing the ASEAN Outlook on the Indo-Pacific, increased collaboration on regional financial crimes, and expanding health and finance linkages introduced at the G20 to the ASEAN region.  Australia provided technical inputs and support alongside the Australian Treasury to key Indonesian Government partners including the Coordinating Ministry of Economic Affairs and the Ministry of Finance.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
